  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef received a permutation P1,P2,…,PNP_1, P_2, \\ldots, P_NP1​,P2​,…,PN​ and also an integer DDD from his good friend Grux, because Grux was afraid he would forget them somewhere. However, since Grux was just playing with the permutation, it was all shuffled, and Chef only likes sorted permutations, so he decided to sort it by performing some swaps.\n\nChef wants to use the integer DDD he just received, so he is only willing to swap two elements of the permutation whenever their absolute difference is exactly DDD. He has limited time, so you should determine the minimum number of swaps he needs to perform to sort the permutation, or tell him that it is impossible to sort it his way.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤201 \\le T \\le 201≤T≤20",
      "1≤N≤200,0001 \\le N \\le 200,0001≤N≤200,000",
      "1≤D≤N1 \\le D \\le N1≤D≤N",
      "1≤Pi≤N1 \\le P_i \\le N1≤Pi​≤N for each valid iii",
      "P1,P2,…,PNP_1, P_2, \\ldots, P_NP1​,P2​,…,PN​ are pairwise distinct",
      "the sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 10610^6106",
      "Subtasks**Subtask #1 (20 points):** D=1D = 1D=1",
      "**Subtask #2 (30 points):**",
      "N≤1,000N \\le 1,000N≤1,000",
      "the sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 10,00010,00010,000"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "The first line of the input contains a single integer TTT denoting the number of test cases. The description of TTT test cases follows.\nThe first line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and DDD.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers P1,P2,…,PNP_1, P_2, \\ldots, P_NP1​,P2​,…,PN​.",
    "outputFormat": "For each test case, print a single line containing one integer ― the minimum number of swaps, or −1-1−1 if it is impossible to sort the permutation.",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n5 2   \n3 4 5 2 1\n5 2    \n4 3 2 1 5",
        "output": "3\n-1",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nExample case 1: Chef can perform the following swaps in this order:\n\nswap the first and fifth element\nswap the third and fifth element\nswap the second and fourth element"
      }
    ]
